    {
      "slug": "reddit-lounge-flagged-account",
      "script": "Expired means expired? I work the front desk at an airport lounge. A woman in a business blazer swiped an expired day-pass, it declined, then said her corporate account meant I should let it slide. I scanned the account number. It existed—flagged, cancelled six months ago—because the company itself complained about someone smuggling four extra guests past a desk agent on one pass. Efficient networking. I read the note aloud: guest attempted to reuse an expired credential, security notified. She went quiet. She was that guest. The system remembered her name. Security walked her out past the free mimosas while her flight started boarding without her. She landed in economy, seat 34E: no lounge, no elite status, just a database with a better memory. How did seat 34E feel?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a three-word question hook, compressed the flagged-account reveal, added one dry aside, and landed on seat 34E while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-fifteen-minute-sign",
      "script": "Free food after fifteen? The diner posted: any table that waits over fifteen minutes for food eats free, no questions asked. Management put it up years ago as marketing; nobody timed anything. I got hired. I time everything. First week, I comped four meals in one shift because the kitchen averaged twenty-two minutes a ticket, not the fifteen promised. My manager said I was being too literal about a sign that was just for show. Apparently, punctuation was optional. I said the sign didn't say for show; it said fifteen minutes. Corporate started asking why one location was comping so much food. On paper, the kitchen caused it, not me. They didn't fix the kitchen. They took the sign down instead. The wait never got shorter. Which cost more: free meals or admitting fifteen minutes was fiction?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, tightened the sign-versus-kitchen escalation, added one dry punctuation aside, and landed on the fifteen-minute fiction while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    }
